<h2>use appendix or supplementary online material (som) a lot</h2>

In fact most of the paper should be there.<br><br>

Look at top journals like Nature, Science, and PNAS. Most of work is in
appendix or supplementary online material (som). It may be 20 or
even 200 pages: all the details, elaborations, and supplementary
analyses, robustness/sensitivity checks etc.<br>

But the actual paper is several pages
only and typically just 1-3 tables/figures. Only the most
important and critical ones that make the point and tell the story;
all the other stuff that is useful but not necessary to make the key
point/tell the story is postponed to appendix/som. We obviously have
info overload, do not overload your readers, be as concise and to the
point as possible. People do not have time to read the stuff that they
do not have to. Readers just want to learn new stuff as quickly and
efficiently as possible. Rare few who want all the details and
intricacies will find them in appendix/som.


<h2>workflow</h2>

not that impossible other way, but seems most productive (at least
for me): 3 main stages:<br><br>

1) conceptualize, figure it out, get the idea formed, ideally do start looking
at the data and literature and get basic things figured out, make a
lot of notes: this can be done at various intervals here and there<br><br>

2) actual production: this one you have to have blocks of uninterrupted
time! something like tens of hours, say 30hrs over Sat and Sun, (with
some sleep between); and then more blocks like that; probably around
50-200hrs, as continuously as possible; indeed best just have like one
or two  full weeks to nail it, say >=80hrs/wk; then take like 2
weeks off!<br><br>

3) finally, polishing, reorganizing, redoing some of it, focusing,
simplifying, etc, like 1st stage can be done here and there<br><br>

set it aside and read in few weeks: if  you have not looked at it for few weeks, you read it with fresh mind and
you will discover many things are not there and not obvious/logical, because as
you wrote it it was all in your head and could not tell earlier something was missing<br><br>

A word on <b>consistency</b>. Yes necessaary in many classes like independent
or directed study or dissertation hours: just cannot do nothing the
whole semester and then crunch it in the final week of the semester,
the reseach will not be great, as it needs time and maturation like
wine.<br>

But with research in general I would favor spentaneity/inspiration over
consistency. Some advise to write like for an hour or few
every day. It does not work for me, not even any limit for a week or
month. I would rather do it when I am inspired. Likewise, I work much
better on what I want to do and I tend to favor it over things
that need to be done (still often really need to do what needs to be
done).<br> 

What I find working best is working hard continuously for some time and then taking time
off. When I was in my 20s and 30s I would even do 2 weeks or longer, I
mean continously like about 80hrs per week. Now in my 40s I tend to
keep it shorter than 2 weeks, a week or 10 days, and then at least few
days break, otherwhise getting literaly sick. 



<h3>do work continuously but not for too long</h3>

<dl>
  <li> warning: thinking/academic work is bad for your body!!
    do a test: sit on laptop for couple hrs continuously (typical to
    get anything done),
    then get up and note how weak you are
  <li> and have enough of these couple hrs blocks over time and you deteriorate greatly in
    general, not just physically, mentally too--sure
    easy to see why working in a mine with a hammer is bad for you, but so
    is thinking/academic work
  <li> thin line: do need uninterrupted blocks of time, eg all Fri,
    all Sat, all Sun at 14hrs per day, BUT then do other stuff like
    outdoors, exercise, social connection--if you do research all the
    time for too long it will damage your brain
  <li> it is actually good not just for happiness, but also for productivity to
    take a break; have like a week or two without looking at it at all,
    and then when you come back you will not just be more productive, but
    also see things you did not see earlier; BUT before you take a break
    wrap up to have it in a decent shape; AND do make notes what are
    the next steps and things TODO when you come back
  <li> let it sit for 2-3 weeks; or even longer, few months (after the class,
    of course); when you come back you see things you have not seen before
    because its all in your head and you do not realize whats on paper and
    whats missing where (your head v paper)
