<h3>paper is useless if held in a drawer, share it
  with the world</h3>


<!-- Not just to impress and boost your CV, but mostly to make it better; -->
<!-- research is a life long pursuit, it takes years or a lifetime to -->
<!-- really figure something  out. And the only way to do that is to work -->
<!-- with your field. -->
<dl>
  <li> Your field does not know about you, unless you
    share. You share in 2 ways only: academic conferences and
    publications--the only way to take your work to the next level
    is through the feedback from your field.
  <li> Again, the top people
    in your narrowly defined field, say about 5-50 people,
    will not be at your school. Sure there will be people in related areas,
    but not exactly precisely in the very narrow area that you are
    in. Almost never you are not doing the exact same narrow area from
    the exact same perspective as scholars at your institution.
  <li> Comments from your narrowly defined field are
    priceless. Again, it is the only way to take your work to the world class
    level. And do not get discouraged, typically you will get
    rejected. I am getting rejected say 70 percent of the time after being
    in the field for 20 years. But you still get the priceless
      comments on how to make it better. Unless you get desk rejected,
    which still gives you some information.
</dl>


<h3>keep your pipeline full</h3>

<dl>
  <li> It takes about 3mo to get comments, even 6mo, write another
  paper and submit, keep it going, do not wait for comments to move on,
    you will be stuck for months.
  <li> *KEEP PIPELINE FULL*, I always have at least 3 papers under review at any given time
</dl>



