  why would x cause y, and not the other way round, or some other z 
  <li> less philosophically and more social sciency can think about and address: causality, endogeneity, self-selection
  <li> reaable ref:  <a href="http://people.bu.edu/tsimcoe/code/Endog-PDW.pdf">http://people.bu.edu/tsimcoe/code/Endog-PDW.pdf</a>
</dl>


<h1>publishing game: editors, reviewers, revise and resubmit</h1>

work smart, not hard, and have some strategy; pointers below:
<dl>
  <!-- <li> Remember the editor makes the decision, not reviewer. -->
  <!-- <li> And in general in later rounds of reviews the edits to paper are lighter and lighter -->
  <li> If some comment really takes you away and makes it into a new paper, say so in response to reviewer!
    Thank her for comments, but say that it will be a new paper, so it
    cannnot be fully accommodated here.
    And then indeed in the future you may write that another new paper. I did few times. Then you have 2 papers 😃
  <li> A great way to deal with changes to paper is to put them into
    appendix or supplementary online material (som). Say in response
    to a reviewer that you took suggestions
  into account and put them in appendix or online supplementary
    material. Can put there
  changes to satisfy one reviewer but avoid dissatisfying the other. Almost
  nobody will read appendix or online supplementary material, so what
  is there does not upset a person, but it can be used to satisfy another
    person. And yes you will actually quite often find reviewers disagreeing.
  <li> Couple ideas how to figure out where to go with the paper: 
    <dl>
      <li> How strong/mild is suggestion?
      <li> Editor is more important than reviewer.
      <li> Some reviewers are much more important than others (some
	are students; some are very senior scholars),
	and so reviews are not equal, some are weighted much more than
	others. 
      <li> In general try to satisfy as much as possible, disagreeing
      risks rejection. Think critically, which reviewer  makes more sense and
	follow that.
      <li> Sometimes do disagree, especially if a person does not make
      sense (it happens often), but always be super polite and respectful. 
      <li> The more senior you become, the more you may disagree; not
      just that you know more, but you risk less, you already have
      many papers published and going on. 
    </dl>
</dl>


