<h3>do start with very small/incremental contribution or even replication only</h3>

Many journals would be interested in such work, eg Heliyon: 
"Any paper reporting scientifically accurate and valuable research,
which adheres to accepted ethical and scientific publishing standards,
will be considered for publication. As such, the journal also
publishes manuscripts reporting negative/null results, incremental
advances, and replication studies"<br><br>

RU has a deal with Elsevier to cover APC (article publishing charge)
BUT do make sure first! (for Heliyon it does not work)


<h3>title and abstract are important</h3>


Want both title and abstract to reflect paper main points as good as
possible. Title and abstract are very important! Many (most?) will only
read abstract, and many will only read the paper solely based on title
and abstract, so in a way title and abstract are actually the most
important parts of the paper. The key info in title and abstract are
 your findings and main points you make, not background info or literature.


<h2>journal choice and rankings</h2>

<dl>
  <li> see rankings: <a href="https://www.scimagojr.com/journalrank.php">scimagojr.com</a>,
  do not submit to top top ones like Nature or Science, not even the
  top general ones in a discipline (unless you have already published
  few articles), but do aim for decent good reputable journals, ie
  SJR>0.5 and IF>1
  <li> most papers (even above 90 percent) get desk rejected by editor and mostly for lack of
  fit, do see what the editors are looking for, read the editorial!
  (fun fact: I have never read one until I wrote one for SIR)
  <li> per fit: see which journal you cite a lot, especialy recent
    studies, or in which journal are the critical papers that you
    cite, again recent ones
  <li> authors that you cite a lot and or those that are closest to
    your study will be your reviewers (gatekeepers/judges of your success)
  <li> pro-tip: co-author with someone who published recently in the
    target journal
